// Package guestbook is this bounded context's facade — the package a
// deployment unit imports to assemble it.
//
// Same rule as greeting's: factories and NOTHING else. No
// `type Guestbook = domain.Guestbook`, no
// `var ErrAnonymousVisitor = domain.ErrAnonymousVisitor`, however
// convenient. The domain sits behind this context's internal/, so a
// consumer that cannot name its types cannot declare its own
// implementation of its ports either.
//
// NewGuestbook takes the driven port the context needs, which means
// only a caller holding one can assemble it — and the only thing that
// holds one is the assembly, which is where contexts are wired
// together. That is deliberate: a peer receives what this context
// returns, it does not build one.
package guestbook

import "<%= peerDomainImport %>"

// NewGuestbook assembles the sign use case over the Welcome port and
// returns it behind its driving port. Each cmd/ main wires it by
// hand — constructor injection, no framework.
func NewGuestbook(welcome domain.Welcome) domain.Guestbook {
	return domain.NewGuestbook(welcome)
}
